Phase 1:
In this phase, I created a Song class and a SinglyLinkedList that can add songs to the end and display the playlist.
A linked list stores elements in separate nodes that point to the next node, while an array stores elements in consecutive positions in memory.
This means a linked list connects its elements using references instead of relying on indexes.

Phase 2:
In this phase, I implemented the Iterable interface and added a custom iterator with a remove() method to delete songs from the playlist.
Unlinking a node updates the next pointer of the previous node to bypass the target element and point straight to the current node.
This allows the iterator to securely delete items while keeping its place in the list via the already-advanced cursor.

Phase 3:
In this phase, I added methods to look up and insert songs at specific spots in the playlist.
Both arrays and linked lists take some time to find a spot in the middle, but arrays are more expensive because they have to shift all the other items over in memory.
A linked list just updates a couple of references (pointers) without needing to move anything else around.

Phase 4:
In this phase, I created a SortedLinkedList that automatically puts songs in order as they are added using a Comparator.
Using a separate class with a Comparator is much better than jamming an addSorted method into a regular list because it keeps the sorting logic separate from the list structure.
This makes the code super flexible—you can easily make lists sorted by title, artist, or duration without having to rewrite or clutter your core list class.

